WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.608
Authorization failures happen when an application does not consistently verify whether an authenticated user is allowed to access a record or perform an action.

2
00:00:09.608 --> 00:00:14.777
Authentication answers who the user is; authorization answers what that user may do.

3
00:00:14.777 --> 00:00:24.206
Privilege escalation can let a normal user perform administrative actions or access data meant for another user, project, contract, or business unit.

4
00:00:24.206 --> 00:00:30.212
In CUI environments, that can turn an application flaw into an information disclosure incident.

5
00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:44.351
Insecure direct object references occur when object identifiers such as record IDs, file names, case numbers, or document references are exposed without checking whether the current user is authorized for the target object.

6
00:00:44.351 --> 00:00:53.580
Access control bypasses often appear in hidden routes, disabled client-side controls, unprotected APIs, and forced browsing.

7
00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:59.357
The server must enforce access decisions because client-side behavior is not a security boundary.

8
00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:03.588
Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrators.

9
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:07.499
Enforce access decisions on the server for every sensitive action.

10
00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:11.860
Use deny-by-default design so access must be explicitly granted.

11
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.571
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include AC.

12
